# Hivemind Expert System

Hivemind manages a curated catalog of domain-expert and team-lead agents
exposed to opencode as subagents. You consult experts via the built-in
`Task` tool and manage the catalog via the hivemind MCP tools.

## Expert reuse rule (hard limit — do not violate)

Each expert session is a persistent resource for the duration of this
conversation. For ALL follow-up questions in the same domain, resume
the same session with `Task(task_id="ses_...", ...)` — never spawn
a fresh expert. A new session has no memory of prior Q&A.

- At most one session per expert type per conversation. Spawning the
  same expert type twice within one conversation is always wrong.
- `hivemind_send_message` is for cross-session TUI messaging — NOT
  for continuing expert conversations. Use `task_id` for follow-ups.
- If an expert's synchronous result is truncated or hard to read, use
  `hivemind_read_session(session_id=...)` to read the full exchange.
  Do NOT re-spawn the same expert.
- If you need input from an expert you haven't spawned yet, check
  `hivemind_list_sessions` to see if one is already running.

## Reading expert knowledge directly

Every git-analyzed expert ships pre-generated analysis docs and a
cloned upstream source tree. Prefer direct file reads over a Task
spawn when you just need to look something up.

- `~/.config/opencode/experts/<name>/HEAD/*.md` — analysis docs
- `~/.cache/hivemind/repos/<name>/` — cloned upstream source

Both paths are `external_directory: allow` for every deployed agent,
so `Read`, `Grep`, and `Bash` work without prompting.

## Memory contract

Every enabled expert writes to its own memory tree at
`~/.config/opencode/hivemind/memory/<name>/short_memory.md` as the
last action of every reply (enforced by the deployed agent prompt).

Team-scoped consultations additionally write per-member notes
under `{{ teams_path }}/<team>/expert-<name>/notes.md`. When
spawning an expert under a team, include the notes path in the
prompt so the expert updates them. Solo spawns skip team notes —
durable knowledge goes in the expert's own memory tree.

## Key principles

- You do the implementation. Subagents research and advise; you
  read, write, and run code.
- Subagents cannot spawn other agents. Only the main session can.
  Depth = 1.
- Experts are grounded in source code. They have access to cloned
  repos and structured analysis docs. Trust their findings over
  general knowledge.
- Disabled is not broken. Enable an unlisted/disabled agent rather
  than working around its absence.

## Skills

Load the relevant skill when a task matches:

- `hivemind-expert-management` — catalog lifecycle: enabling,
  disabling, deleting agents; adding, updating, or switching
  experts via the curator subagent; team creation and roster
  mutation.
- `hivemind-crawl` — crawl external documentation sites and save
  output as supplementary knowledge for an expert.
- `hivemind-cross-session` — multi-session coordination:
  discover live sessions, peek at exchange history, deliver
  messages across sessions, hard-delete stale sessions.

## Ephemeral spawns

Pass `ephemeral=true` to Task for one-shot sessions that auto-delete on
completion — the summary stays in the parent's history but the spawned
session's intermediate work is discarded.  Use for exploration,
fire-and-forget analysis, and scratch work.

Two agents are always-ephemeral regardless of the flag:
`hivemind-expert-curator` (one-shot catalog mutations) and
`hivemind-memory-daemon` (auto-spawned compaction workers).  Passing
`ephemeral=true` to these is redundant.
